<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-partial-variation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Partial Variation</title>
    
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>1.0 Numbers and Algebra</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>1.5 Formulae and Variations</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>Solve combined, joint and partial variations in different situations</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>11</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>40 minutes</p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

 <axiom component="lesson-plan">
    <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery: Exploring Fixed and Variable Costs</title>
    <dl>
        <li>
            <title>Suggested Time</title>
            <p>15 minutes</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <title>Objective</title>
            <p>
               Enable learners to investigate a community water delivery scenario, identify patterns in the delivery charges, and discover how a fixed charge and a variable charge combine to form a linear relationship.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <title>Teacher's Role</title>
            <p>
               Organize learners into small groups and present the community water delivery scenario. Encourage learners to discuss why a fixed delivery fee is charged before completing the table and identifying patterns. Guide learners using questions such as: "Which part of the charge remains the same?" and "Which part changes with distance?" Allow learners to formulate the equation themselves before introducing the formal concept of partial variation.
            </p>
        </li>
    </dl>
</axiom>

  <activity>
    <statement>

        <p><em>Work in groups of 3–4.</em></p>

        <p><em>Scenario: Community Water Delivery</em></p>

        <p>
            A water vendor delivers clean water to homes in a community. To cover transport costs, the vendor charges:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li><em>Ksh 100</em> as a fixed delivery fee.</li>
            <li><em>Ksh 50</em> for every kilometre travelled.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            Your group has been asked to help the vendor estimate delivery charges for customers living different distances away.
        </p>

        <p>
            Let <m>x</m> represent the distance travelled (km) and <m>y</m> represent the total delivery charge (Ksh).
        </p>

        <p><em>(a) Explore the Delivery Charges</em></p>

        <ol>
            <li>Discuss why the vendor charges a fixed delivery fee before charging per kilometre.</li>
            <li>Predict the delivery charge for a home that is <em>12 km</em> away.</li>
            <li>Complete the table below.</li>
        </ol>

        <table>
            <tabular halign="center">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Customer</cell>
                    <cell>Distance (<m>x</m> km)</cell>
                    <cell>Working</cell>
                    <cell>Total Charge (<m>y</m> Ksh)</cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>Home A</cell>
                    <cell>0</cell>
                    <cell>100 + 50(0)</cell>
                    <cell>100</cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>Home B</cell>
                    <cell>2</cell>
                    <cell>100 + 50(2)</cell>
                    <cell>200</cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>Home C</cell>
                    <cell>5</cell>
                    <cell>100 + 50(5)</cell>
                    <cell>350</cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>Home D</cell>
                    <cell>12</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>Home E</cell>
                    <cell>20</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>Home F</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell>1,100</cell>
                </row>

            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p><em>(b) Discover the Relationship</em></p>

        <ol>
            <li>What pattern do you notice between the distance travelled and the total delivery charge?</li>

            <li>Write an equation relating the total charge <m>y</m> to the distance <m>x</m>.</li>

            <li>Rewrite your equation in the form <m>y = mx + c</m>.</li>

            <li>What does:
                <ul>
                    <li>the gradient (<m>m</m>) represent?</li>
                    <li>the constant (<m>c</m>) represent?</li>
                </ul>
            </li>

        </ol>

        <p><em>(c) Represent the Relationship</em></p>

        <ol>
            <li>Draw a graph of your equation.</li>

            <li>Use your graph to estimate the delivery charge for a customer living <em>15 km</em> away.</li>

            <li>Compare your graph with another group. Discuss any similarities or differences.</li>
        </ol>

        <p><em>(d) Solve a Community Problem</em></p>

        <p>
            Another water vendor charges <em>Ksh 70 per kilometre</em> and does not charge a fixed delivery fee.
        </p>

        <ol type="a">
            <li>Write an equation for the second vendor.</li>

            <li>For what distances is the first vendor cheaper? Show your working.</li>

            <li>At what distance do both vendors charge the same amount?</li>

            <li>If you were advising a family living:
                <ul>
                    <li>3 km away, which vendor would you recommend?</li>
                    <li>18 km away, which vendor would you recommend?</li>
                </ul>
                Explain your answers.
            </li>

        </ol>

    </statement>
</activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
    <title>Structured Instruction</title>
    <dl>
        <li>
            <title>Suggested Time</title>
            <p>10 minutes</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <title>Objective</title>
            <p>
               Consolidate learners' understanding of partial variation and connect their discoveries to the general equation <m>y = mx + c</m>
            </p> </li>
        <li>
            <title>Teacher's Role</title>
            <p>
                Build on learners' observations from the activity by introducing the equation <m>y = mx + c</m>. Explain that <m>m</m> represents the rate at which the delivery charge increases for every kilometre travelled, while <m>c</m> represents the fixed delivery fee charged before the journey begins. Refer to the completed table and graph to show how both quantities can be identified from real-life data. Encourage learners to connect this model to other situations involving a fixed amount together with a varying amount.
            </p>
        </li>
    </dl>
</axiom>

    
<insight>

    <title>Key Takeaway</title>

    <p>
        Partial variation reminds us that many real-life costs are made up of two parts:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>a fixed cost for accessing a service, and</li>
        <li>a variable cost based on how much of the service you use.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Understanding this idea helps you interpret bills, compare services, and make better financial decisions.
    </p>

</insight>

<p>
        In the activity, the total water delivery charge consisted of two parts:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>a fixed delivery fee of <em>Ksh 100</em>, and</li>
        <li><em>Ksh 50</em> for every kilometre travelled.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Many services in everyday life work in the same way. For example, an electricity or water bill usually includes a fixed monthly standing charge plus an additional cost based on the number of units consumed.
    </p>

    <p>
        These situations can all be modelled using the equation:
    </p>

    <md>y = mx + c</md>

    <ul>
        <li><m>y</m> = Total bill or cost</li>
        <li><m>x</m> = Number of units used</li>
        <li><m>m</m> = Cost per unit (the rate)</li>
        <li><m>c</m> = Fixed monthly standing charge</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Other examples of partial variation include transport fares with a booking fee, internet subscriptions, and mobile phone plans that combine a monthly fee with usage charges.
    </p>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>

    <statement>

        <p>
            The Kenya Power and Lighting company(KPLC) charges a fixed monthly standing charge of
            <em>Ksh 500</em> plus <em>Ksh 20</em> for every kilowatt-hour (kWh)
            of electricity used.
        </p>

        <p>
            If a household uses <em>150 kWh</em>, calculate the total electricity bill.
        </p>

    </statement>

    <solution>

        <ol>

            <li>
                Identify the values:
                <ul>
                    <li><m>m = 20</m></li>
                    <li><m>c = 500</m></li>
                    <li><m>x = 150</m></li>
                </ul>
            </li>

            <li>
                Write the equation:
                <md>y = mx + c</md>
            </li>

            <li>
                Substitute the values:
                <md>y = 20(150) + 500</md>
            </li>

            <li>
                Simplify:
                <md>y = 3000 + 500 = 3500</md>
            </li>

        </ol>

        <p>
            Therefore, the total electricity bill is Ksh <alert>3,500.</alert>
        </p>

    </solution>

</example>

<example>

    <statement>

        <p>
            An internet service provider like Safaricom charges a fixed monthly subscription
            of <em>Ksh 2,000</em> plus <em>Ksh 50</em> for every extra GB of data used.
        </p>

        <p>
            If you use <em>30 GB</em> of extra data, calculate the total monthly bill.
        </p>

    </statement>

    <solution>

        <ol>

            <li>
                Identify the values:
                <ul>
                    <li><m>m = 50</m></li>
                    <li><m>c = 2000</m></li>
                    <li><m>x = 30</m></li>
                </ul>
            </li>

            <li>
                Write the equation:
                <md>y = mx + c</md>
            </li>

            <li>
                Substitute the values:
                <md>y = 50(30) + 2000</md>
            </li>

            <li>
                Simplify:
                <md>y = 1500 + 2000 = 3500</md>
            </li>

        </ol>

        <p>
            <alert>Therefore, the monthly internet bill is Ksh 3,500.</alert>
        </p>

    </solution>

</example>


<example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A water drilling company charges a fixed mobilization fee of 50,000 Ksh and an additional 2,500 Ksh for every meter drilled. 
                Write an equation for the total cost (<m>C</m>) in terms of the depth (<m>d</m>) in meters. 
                Find the cost of drilling a 100-meter borehole.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The fixed cost (<m>b</m>) is 50,000 Ksh. The rate (<m>k</m>) is 2,500 Ksh/m.
                The equation is: 
                <md>C = 2500d + 50000</md>
                For <m>d = 100</m>:
                <md>C = 2500(100) + 50000 = 250,000 + 50,000 = 300,000 \text{ Ksh}</md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A salesperson earns a basic salary of 20,000 Ksh per month plus a commission of 500 Ksh for every item sold. 
                In March, they sold 15 items. Calculate their total earnings for that month.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Total Earnings (<m>E</m>) = Basic Salary + (Commission per item × Number of items)
                <md>E = 20000 + 500n</md>
                For <m>n = 15</m>:
                <md>E = 20000 + 500(15) = 20000 + 7500 = 27,500 \text{ Ksh}</md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A car rental agency charges a flat fee of 3,000 Ksh plus 50 Ksh for every kilometer driven. 
                If a customer paid 5,500 Ksh, how many kilometers did they drive?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The equation is <m>5500 = 50x + 3000</m>, where <m>x</m> is the distance.
                <md>5500 - 3000 = 50x</md>
                <md>2500 = 50x</md>
                <md>x = \frac{2500}{50} = 50 \text{ km}</md>
                The customer drove 50 kilometers.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-car-rental-example">
                <caption>Partial variation graph for car rental: <m>y = 50x + 3000</m>, where y is total cost and x is kilometers driven.</caption>
                <image width="100%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}
                        \begin{axis}[
                            grid=both,
                            minor grid style={draw=gray!15, line width=0.2pt},
                            major grid style={draw=gray!40, line width=0.4pt},
                            minor tick num=1,
                            xtick distance=10,
                            ytick distance=1000,
                            axis lines=left,
                            ylabel={Cost ($y$) Ksh},
                            xlabel={Distance ($x$) km},
                            xmin=0, xmax=60, ymin=0, ymax=6000,
                            width=6.5cm, height=7cm,
                            tick label style={font=\small},
                            label style={font=\small}
                        ]
                        \addplot[red, samples=2, domain=0:55] {50*x + 3000};
                        \addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue, mark size=2pt] coordinates {(0,3000) (10,3500) (30,4500) (50,5500)};
                        \end{axis}
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A private tutor charges a base fee of 1,200 Ksh for a home visit, plus 800 Ksh for every hour of teaching. 
                    If the tutor spends <m>h</m> hours at a student's house, write the equation for the total cost (<m>C</m>). 
                    How much will a 3-hour session cost?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    The fixed cost is <m>b = 1200</m> and the rate is <m>k = 800</m>.
                    The equation is: <md>C = 800h + 1200</md>
                    For <m>h = 3</m>:
                    <md>C = 800(3) + 1200 = 2400 + 1200 = 3,600 \text{ Ksh}</md>
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-tutor-fee-exercise">
                    <caption>Partial variation graph for tutor fees: <m>C = 800h + 1200</m>, showing the base visit fee of 1,200 Ksh.</caption>
                    <image width="100%">
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}
                            \begin{axis}[
                                grid=both,
                                minor grid style={draw=gray!15, line width=0.2pt},
                                major grid style={draw=gray!40, line width=0.4pt},
                                minor tick num=1,
                                xtick distance=1,
                                ytick distance=1000,
                                axis lines=left,
                                ylabel={Cost ($C$) Ksh},
                                xlabel={Hours ($h$)},
                                xmin=0, xmax=6, ymin=0, ymax=6500,
                                width=6.5cm, height=7cm,
                                tick label style={font=\small},
                                label style={font=\small}
                            ]
                            \addplot[red, samples=2, domain=0:5] {800*x + 1200};
                            \addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue, mark size=2pt] coordinates {(0,1200) (1,2000) (2,2800) (3,3600)};
                            \end{axis}
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In a partial variation, <m>y</m> is the sum of a constant and a value that varies as <m>x</m>. 
                    If <m>y = 17</m> when <m>x = 3</m> and <m>y = 27</m> when <m>x = 8</m>, 
                    find the value of <m>y</m> when <m>x = 12</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Using <m>y = kx + b</m>:
                    <ol>
                        <li><m>17 = 3k + b</m></li>
                        <li><m>27 = 8k + b</m></li>
                    </ol>
                    Subtract (1) from (2) to get <m>5k = 10</m>, which means <m>k = 2</m>.
                    Substitute <m>k = 2</m> into (1): 
                    <md>17 = 3(2) + b \text{ so } 17 = 6 + b \text{ giving } b = 11</md>
                    The law is <m>y = 2x + 11</m>. For <m>x = 12</m>:
                    <md>y = 2(12) + 11 = 24 + 11 = 35</md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A certain parking lot in Nairobi charges 200 Ksh for entry and an additional 50 Ksh for every hour parked. 
                    If a driver paid a total of 550 Ksh, for how many hours did they park?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Let <m>h</m> be the hours.
                    <md>550 = 50h + 200</md>
                    Subtract 200 from both sides:
                    <md>350 = 50h</md>
                    Which means:
                    <md>h = \frac{350}{50} = 7 \text{ hours}</md>
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-parking-lot-exercise">
                    <caption>Partial variation graph for parking lot: <m>y = 50h + 200</m>, showing entry fee of 200 Ksh plus hourly rate.</caption>
                    <image width="100%">
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}
                            \begin{axis}[
                                grid=both,
                                minor grid style={draw=gray!15, line width=0.2pt},
                                major grid style={draw=gray!40, line width=0.4pt},
                                minor tick num=1,
                                xtick distance=1,
                                ytick distance=100,
                                axis lines=left,
                                ylabel={Cost ($y$) Ksh},
                                xlabel={Hours ($h$)},
                                xmin=0, xmax=10, ymin=0, ymax=600,
                                width=6.5cm, height=7cm,
                                tick label style={font=\small},
                                label style={font=\small}
                            ]
                            \addplot[red, samples=2, domain=0:9] {50*x + 200};
                            \addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue, mark size=2pt] coordinates {(0,200) (2,300) (5,450) (7,550)};
                            \end{axis}
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The cost of producing school jerseys involves a fixed setup cost for the printing machine and a variable cost per jersey. 
                    Producing 10 jerseys costs 15,000 Ksh, while producing 25 jerseys costs 30,000 Ksh. 
                    Find the fixed setup cost.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Let <m>C = kn + b</m>:
                    <ol>
                        <li><m>15000 = 10k + b</m></li>
                        <li><m>30000 = 25k + b</m></li>
                    </ol>
                    Subtract (1) from (2) to get <m>15k = 15000</m>, so <m>k = 1000</m>.
                    Substitute <m>k = 1000</m> into (1): 
                    <md>15000 = 10(1000) + b \text{ which gives } 15000 = 10000 + b</md>
                    Therefore, <m>b = 5,000</m>. The fixed setup cost is 5,000 Ksh.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A freight company charges a flat insurance fee of 2,500 Ksh per shipment and a transport fee of 150 Ksh per kilogram. 
                    Calculate the total cost of shipping a crate weighing 45 kilograms.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Total Cost (<m>T</m>) = <m>150w + 2500</m>, where <m>w</m> is weight.
                    <md>T = 150(45) + 2500</md>
                    <md>T = 6750 + 2500 = 9,250 \text{ Ksh}</md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

   <axiom component="lesson-plan">
    <title>Differentiation</title>

    <table>
        <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
        <tabular halign="left">
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                <cell>Strategy &amp; Learning Experience</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
                <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                <cell>
                    <line>Continue using counters, tiles, or drawings to separate the fixed amount from the changing amount.</line>
                    <line>Provide partially completed tables and ask learners to identify the fixed value and the repeated increase.</line>
                    <line>Use everyday examples such as taxi fares, electricity bills, and mobile money charges before introducing algebraic notation.</line>
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
                <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                <cell>
                    <line>The core lesson activities as described above.</line>
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
                <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                <cell>
                    <line>Challenge learners to determine the equation of a partial variation relationship from two points without being given the fixed amount.</line>
                    <line>Ask learners to compare direct and partial variation graphs and explain the significance of the y-intercept and gradient.</line>
                    <line>Provide modelling tasks requiring learners to create their own real-life examples of partial variation and justify the values of <m>k</m> and <m>b</m>.</line>
                </cell>
            </row>

        </tabular>
    </table>
</axiom>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
